00:53:32.240 --> 00:53:34.040
Science question that intrigued me.

00:53:34.040 --> 00:53:36.393
This is from Autumn, 7autumn5 on Twitter.

00:53:37.450 --> 00:53:42.450
Why does warm water feel so
much better than warm air?

00:53:42.880 --> 00:53:45.340
A really hot shower is awesome,

00:53:45.340 --> 00:53:49.430
but if I were in a 100˚
room I'd be uncomfortable.

00:53:49.430 --> 00:53:50.836
It's a great question.

00:53:50.836 --> 00:53:53.030
I looked up just to make sure

00:53:53.030 --> 00:53:55.410
that the way I was thinking about this,

00:53:55.410 --> 00:53:56.370
and I didn't have it...

00:53:56.370 --> 00:53:58.910
I didn't completely
understand what was going on.

00:53:58.910 --> 00:54:02.490
I think a good place
to start is let's start

00:54:02.490 --> 00:54:03.970
with a lower temperature.

00:54:03.970 --> 00:54:05.650
If you're outside and it's 70...

00:54:05.650 --> 00:54:10.200
Well, 70˚ is the approximate temperature

00:54:10.200 --> 00:54:14.450
at which a human feels
comfortable in the air.

00:54:14.450 --> 00:54:16.180
If the air is 70˚ it's like

00:54:16.180 --> 00:54:18.300
most people feel pretty comfortable.

00:54:18.300 --> 00:54:21.200
That is based on the idea that your body

00:54:21.200 --> 00:54:24.110
is doing all these different
metabolic processes

00:54:24.110 --> 00:54:25.220
that is generating heat

00:54:25.220 --> 00:54:26.490
and you're dissipating the heat

00:54:26.490 --> 00:54:27.640
through a number of processes

00:54:27.640 --> 00:54:30.770
like the blood flowing close
to the surface of your skin

00:54:30.770 --> 00:54:32.240
so you give off a little bit of heat.

00:54:32.240 --> 00:54:34.300
You're breathing and there's hot air

00:54:34.300 --> 00:54:36.310
that's coming out of you,
and/or your sweating,

00:54:36.310 --> 00:54:38.370
you're perspiring as you're
cooling yourself down.

00:54:38.370 --> 00:54:41.070
Don't forget the gold standard farting.

00:54:41.070 --> 00:54:43.310
Farting releases a lot of-

00:54:43.310 --> 00:54:44.541
Heat, heat.

00:54:44.541 --> 00:54:47.420
Methane as they say
across the pond and heat.

00:54:47.420 --> 00:54:50.698
It turns out that the reason
you feel comfortable at 70˚-

00:54:50.698 --> 00:54:52.350
It's like a blowtorch down there.

00:54:52.350 --> 00:54:56.320
Is because all these
things that are happening

00:54:56.320 --> 00:55:01.320
in your body is in a good state
of equilibrium at about 70˚.

00:55:01.663 --> 00:55:02.496
It's not equally.

00:55:02.496 --> 00:55:03.430
It's actually cooling.

00:55:03.430 --> 00:55:06.364
There is a gradient not equilibrium.

00:55:06.364 --> 00:55:08.870
Well, when I say equilibrium I mean,

00:55:08.870 --> 00:55:11.460
I'm talking about the process
of you dissipating heat

00:55:11.460 --> 00:55:14.020
is at a place where your body
is doing it most efficiently,

00:55:14.020 --> 00:55:16.250
and you're not having to do
it too little or too much.

00:55:16.250 --> 00:55:17.620
You're not having to reserve the heat,

00:55:17.620 --> 00:55:20.282
and you're also not having
to cool yourself down

00:55:20.282 --> 00:55:21.900
and make yourself uncomfortable.

00:55:21.900 --> 00:55:25.080
The body is doing this in
a most efficient manner.

00:55:25.080 --> 00:55:27.080
I think what you're saying
is, the rate of cooling-

00:55:27.080 --> 00:55:28.420
Is what makes you feel good.

00:55:28.420 --> 00:55:29.400
Makes you feel good.

00:55:29.400 --> 00:55:32.213
Because if it gets cooler,
if it gets down to 65,

00:55:33.440 --> 00:55:36.150
it starts to suck heat out of
your body to a point where-

00:55:36.150 --> 00:55:37.300
Now, you've got to put clothes on.

00:55:37.300 --> 00:55:39.540
Now I've got to do
something to alter this.

00:55:39.540 --> 00:55:42.160
I'm getting signals that I'm uncomfortable

00:55:42.160 --> 00:55:43.760
because this could lead to...

00:55:43.760 --> 00:55:45.160
If this continues forever-

00:55:45.160 --> 00:55:45.993
I could die.

00:55:45.993 --> 00:55:46.826
I could die.

